{"id": 20, "ts": 1780631992.3728323, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "sarah_mitchell", "message": "I\u2019m less convinced that Candidate\u202fD or Candidate\u202fB can be safely recommended at this stage because we have no concrete evidence of how they actually cooperate with crew members. James confirmed there are no observations of D\u2019s crew\u2011cooperation or leadership, and Emily noted there are no documented examples of B\u2019s crew\u2011cooperation despite the concerns about his \u201cpretentious\u201d tone and \u201cnasty remarks.\u201d\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fC brings several safety\u2011critical strengths that are directly documented: he is **100\u202f% reliable**, **keeps calm in a crisis** (showing strong stress resilience), and **makes correct decisions quickly** (high decision quality). Moreover, he **creates a positive atmosphere with his crew**, which suggests effective crew cooperation and professional communication in practice.\nThe remaining concerns for C\u2014difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric style, and limited willingness to further education\u2014are important, but they appear less immediately hazardous than a lack of proven crew\u2011cooperation or a weak numerical memory in long\u2011haul operations. With targeted crew\u2011resource\u2011training and professional development, these issues can be mitigated.\nGiven the current evidence, I recommend we move forward with **Candidate\u202fC** as the best overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
